A||||10||beer_stud_21||computer outputs "error 12" 10+ times||||1||
Z||000000||beer_stud_21||02-05-2001||03:12 AM||||Hey all,<P>Just got my first fiero yesterday.   I'm trying to get it running and have checked the ecm for trouble codes.  The only code it gives is error 12 which as i understand it is normal to show that the computer is operating correctly.  The only problem is that instead of showing 3 times and stopping it keeps going for 10+ times.  I finally just shut the key off.  Any ideas?<P>Thanks a BUNCH all, this forum is awsome!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>-Marlin<BR>"Marla" 85GT/auto.  Currently in rough shape||||reg||1
Z||000001||2birds||02-05-2001||07:04 AM||||Be happy. That's what it's supposed to do.  It starts with the 12 code 3 times, then shows each trouble code (if any), then the 12 code 3 times again.  Then it starts all over again.  And it keeps going until you shut it off or pull the jumper wire.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||02-05-2001||10:08 AM||||Exactly right! If there are no trouble codes stored, it will display a code 12 repeatedly.  <P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||14
Z||000003||beer_stud_21||02-05-2001||01:03 PM||||OK, thats good.  BUT......   I have the sensor disconnected from the air filter box(it was completely rusted to hell) and the engine won't run.  The guy I bought it from said it was the electronic part down inside the distributor.  A mechanic friend of mine told me that if the Dist had problems that it would show up on the computer.  What should I do if my computer says everything is fine when it probably isnt?<P>Thanks again..<P>-Marlin||||reg||1
Z||000004||ChadMan||02-05-2001||01:09 PM||||A bad ignition module in the distributor won't set a code -- the car just stops running.  It's a cheap part and easy to get to, so you might as well replace it.  Maybe you'll get lucky and it will fire right up?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fieroboy1||02-05-2001||02:25 PM||||Beer stud...check for spark at the plugs.  I f you don't have any it can be one of three things.  Either the coil is bad, the module in the distributor is bad, or the pick up coil in the distributor was bad.  I had a problem with an 86 se i had, there was spark from the main coil, but i had no spark at the plugs. Changed the module, still no spark.  Turned out to be the pick up coil in the distributor. Changing that is a real pain. If you have spark from the main coil and none at the plugs, do yourself a huge favor and just change the whole distributor.  Chances are the one you have could use a replacement anyways.||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||02-05-2001||02:34 PM||||Code 12 is an error, but a "normal" error. It means no distributor reference pulses which is correct when the engine is not running. If the ignition module dies, the engine stops and the code you get is: Code 12. That's why the SES/CE lamp is on when you turn the key on. Not real useful information, I know.... ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||02-05-2001||05:50 PM||||The sensor on the air box is the MAT. it should set a code if it's not plugged in. You need to repair/replace all damaged sensors before doing any other work. Bad MAT or coolant temp will screw up how the engine runs.||||reg||1
Z||000008||2birds||02-05-2001||09:59 PM||||If you know the coil is good, I'd rebuild the distributor next.  Rebuilding your distributor is about the simplest thing to do beyond plugs and oil changes on a V-6 Fiero, IMHO.  Unless the previous owner was a Fiero nut, it probably needs it anyway.  The only special tool I needed was a punch for the drive gear roll pin.  <BR>Replace the pickup coil first, since it's cheap (&lt;$10), and probably rotten anyway.  Get a new distributor tube O-ring, and fix the oil leak you probably have, too.  I cleaned the driveshaft up with some 400 grit, and the whole thing goes back together reall easily.  Put on a new cap and rotor now and see if you have spark now.<BR>The ignition module costs a bit more, but that's probably the next step, and it doesn't require removal of the distributor.  Real straight forward piece to replace, and polarity doesn't matter for the pickup coil leads if you mix them up.||||reg||1
Z||000009||beer_stud_21||02-06-2001||12:56 AM||||First of all: I want to thank all you guys for helping me out.  I'm not much of a mechanic yet and i'm doing this stuff ultra cheap-o so all your advise is priceless to me.  Now I got a few things I need clarified. <P>A) Can I rebuild my distributor like 2birds is saying for just the cost of the o-ring, ignition module and that other part.  If that the case I will go get those and start on that right away. (do I need fiero specific parts?)<P>B) Should I be worried about my computer being toast if it doesnt set a code for the MAT sensor.  My air filter assembly was totally shot to hell so i disconnected the sensor when i tore out the filter canister. <P>Thats it for now..<P>-Marlin<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||2birds||02-06-2001||12:39 PM||||First let me qualify my advice.  If you don't have access to basic tools, a place to work on it, and the basic knowledge of how to use said tools and how things are assembled and disassembled, you need to find someone who does.  <BR>I grew up tinkering with cars with my dad, and I've been maintaining and repairing submarine nuclear power plants in one form or another for the last 15+ years, so I have no doubts about my ability to fix most of the things on my Fiero, especially since I've owned and maintained it for almost 13 years.  <BR>I also know there are some things I wouldn't attempt to do, mostly from lack of facilities or the time to have my car down for work.  I started small and worked my way up to bigger things, and accumulated lots of special tools and knowledge as I went. I paid to have my clutch done the first time, since I lived in an apartment and couldn't afford to lose my car for a couple days, but now I have a house with a garage, and a second car, so I'm a bit more willing to try a more difficult repair.  For example, anything's fair game on the Fiero, but I won't touch things like the engine management system or transmission in my wife's 97 Thunderbird, because of the complexity and special tools required.  That and I'd ever hear the end of it if I screwed something up.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>Sometimes the cost of fixing what you screw up is more than it would have cost to get help.  Cheap is great, but at what overall cost?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 02-06-2001).]||||reg||1
